12.10 Remove items from a list
Open the page, type something in the second row’s input, then click Delete next to the first item. Which row’s typed text disappears?
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
  &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;x-for Without Key&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/alpinejs@3.14.1/dist/cdn.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Remove items from a list&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Type something in the second input field, then click Delete next to the first item.
     Which row's input value disappears?&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;div x-data="{ items: ['Alpha', 'Beta', 'Gamma'] }"&gt;
    &lt;ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0;"&gt;
      &lt;template x-for="item in items"&gt;
        &lt;li&gt;
          &lt;span x-text="item"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;input type="text" :placeholder="'notes for ' + item"&gt;
          &lt;button @click="items.splice(items.indexOf(item), 1)"&gt;Delete&lt;/button&gt;
        &lt;/li&gt;
      &lt;/template&gt;
    &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;






NoteShow explanation





The bug is the missing :key binding on x-for. Without a key, Alpine matches old DOM nodes to new data items purely by position. After the first item is removed, every remaining item shifts up by one position. Alpine reuses the existing DOM nodes in order rather than creating new ones, which means the &lt;input&gt; that belonged to the second item stays in place but is now labeled as the first item. Any typed content appears to have moved to the wrong row.
Shows: why :key matters and how Alpine uses it to match DOM nodes to data items by identity rather than by index, allowing it to move or remove the correct node.
To find it: type in the second input, delete the first item, and watch which input retains the typed value. Add :key="item" to the &lt;template&gt; tag (or use a unique id if items can repeat) so Alpine tracks each DOM node correctly.

12.11 Increment a counter using an external object
Open the page and click Increment several times. Does the counter change?
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
  &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;External State Mutation&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;script&gt;
    const config = { count: 0 };
  &lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/alpinejs@3.14.1/dist/cdn.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Increment a counter using an external object&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Click the button. Does the counter update?&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;div x-data="config"&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Count: &lt;span x-text="count"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;button @click="config.count++"&gt;Increment&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;






NoteShow explanation





The bug is config.count++. When Alpine sees x-data="config", it copies the properties of config into a new reactive proxy and uses that proxy to drive the template. Clicking the button updates config.count on the original plain object, which is no longer connected to Alpine’s proxy. The proxy still holds the old value, so the display never changes.
Shows: that x-data takes a snapshot of its argument’s properties at initialization time. Mutations to the original object after that point are invisible to Alpine. Reactive updates must go through the proxy — meaning you use the bare property name (count++) inside an Alpine expression, not a reference to the external object.
To find it: add console.log(config.count) to the click handler. If it increments but the display doesn’t, the external object and the proxy have diverged. Change config.count++ to count++.

12.12 Close a panel by pressing Escape
Open the page, click somewhere inside the panel, and press Escape. Does the panel close?
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
  &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Keyboard Event Target&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;script defer src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/alpinejs@3.14.1/dist/cdn.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;h1&gt;Close a panel by pressing Escape&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;p&gt;Click anywhere inside the panel, then press Escape. Does it close?&lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;div x-data="{ open: true }"&gt;
    &lt;div x-show="open"
         @keydown.escape="open = false"
         style="padding: 1em; border: 1px solid black;"&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;This panel is open. Press Escape to close it.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;button x-show="!open" @click="open = true"&gt;Reopen&lt;/button&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;






NoteShow explanation





The bug is placing @keydown.escape on a &lt;div&gt; that has no tabindex attribute. Keyboard events are dispatched only to elements that can receive focus. Plain &lt;div&gt; elements are not focusable by default, so they never appear in the keyboard event path and the @keydown listener never fires, no matter how many times you press Escape.
Shows: the connection between focusability and keyboard events, and two ways Alpine provides to listen for window-level keyboard events:

Add tabindex="0" to the &lt;div&gt; to make it focusable, then call .focus() when the panel opens.
Replace @keydown.escape with @keydown.window.escape, which attaches the listener to the window so it fires regardless of which element has focus.

To find it: open DevTools, click inside the panel, and check the Elements panel to see if the &lt;div&gt; is focused (a blue outline or the :focus pseudo-class). If it isn’t focusable, keyboard events skip it.

